/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · Judges chapters 6-10
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("judges", [
{n:6, name:"The Call of Gideon", seal:{ref:"Judges 6:12",verse:"And the angel of the LORD appeared unto him, and said unto him, The LORD is with thee, thou mighty man of valour."},
 facts:["Did you know Gideon was threshing wheat in a winepress? A winepress sits low in the ground, so it hid the wheat from Midianite raiders, but it was a terrible place to thresh, since the wind could not blow the chaff away.","Did you know the sign of the fleece was Gideon's idea, not God's? God had already promised to be with him, yet He still answered the fleece test twice.","Did you know Gideon was given a new name after he tore down Baal's altar? His father called him Jerubbaal, meaning let Baal plead against him."],
 questions:[
  {q:"How many years did the LORD deliver Israel into the hand of Midian?",options:["Seven years","Forty years","Eighteen years","Three years"],a:0,ref:"Judges 6:1",verse:"And the children of Israel did evil in the sight of the LORD: and the LORD delivered them into the hand of Midian seven years.",insight:"Seven years of oppression came because Israel did evil in the sight of the LORD. God lets consequences run their course, but He never stops watching His people."},
  {q:"Where did the Israelites hide from the Midianites?",options:["In dens, caves, and strong holds in the mountains","In the cities of the plain","In the land of Egypt","In the ships of Tarshish"],a:0,ref:"Judges 6:2",verse:"And the hand of Midian prevailed against Israel: and because of the Midianites the children of Israel made them the dens which are in the mountains, and caves, and strong holds.",insight:"The mountains became Israel's refuge because the land had become unsafe. When fear drives you into hiding, it is a sign that something needs to change."},
  {q:"Which peoples came up against Israel like enemies of the harvest?",options:["The Midianites, the Amalekites, and the children of the east","The Philistines, the Moabites, and the Ammonites","The Canaanites, the Hittites, and the Jebusites","The Syrians, the Edomites, and the Zidonians"],a:0,ref:"Judges 6:3",verse:"And so it was, when Israel had sown, that the Midianites came up, and the Amalekites, and the children of the east, even they came up against them;",insight:"The enemies came every harvest to destroy Israel's increase. The devil still targets the harvest moments of your life, the times when God is bringing increase."},
  {q:"What did the raiders leave behind for Israel to eat?",options:["No sustenance at all, neither sheep, nor ox, nor ass","Plenty of corn and wine","Half of every harvest","The gleanings of the fields"],a:0,ref:"Judges 6:4",verse:"And they encamped against them, and destroyed the increase of the earth, till thou come unto Gaza, and left no sustenance for Israel, neither sheep, nor ox, nor ass.",insight:"They left nothing, not even one sheep or donkey. Total loss is meant to drive God's people to total dependence on Him."},
  {q:"How are the Midianites and their allies described when they came up?",options:["They came as grasshoppers for multitude, with camels without number","They came as lions roaring for prey","They came as locusts darkening the sun","They came as a flood covering the land"],a:0,ref:"Judges 6:5",verse:"For they came up with their cattle and their tents, and they came as grasshoppers for multitude; for both they and their camels were without number: and they entered into the land to destroy it.",insight:"They were compared to grasshoppers, countless and devouring. But numbers never impressed God; He would soon defeat that swarm with only three hundred men."},
  {q:"What did Israel do when they were greatly impoverished by Midian?",options:["They cried unto the LORD","They fled into Egypt","They made a league with Midian","They hid their gold in the caves"],a:0,ref:"Judges 6:6",verse:"And Israel was greatly impoverished because of the Midianites; and the children of Israel cried unto the LORD.",insight:"Poverty finally taught them prayer. God often lets the wallet run empty so the heart will finally look up."},
  {q:"Whom did the LORD send to Israel when they cried out?",options:["A prophet","A king","An army from Egypt","A judge from Gilead"],a:0,ref:"Judges 6:8",verse:"That the LORD sent a prophet unto the children of Israel, which said unto them, Thus saith the LORD God of Israel, I brought you up from Egypt, and brought you forth out of the house of bondage;",insight:"Before God sent a deliverer, He sent a preacher to explain why they suffered. God wants us to understand our trouble, not just escape it."},
  {q:"Where was Gideon when the angel of the LORD appeared to him?",options:["Threshing wheat by the winepress in Ophrah","Plowing a field in Bethlehem","Tending sheep on Mount Gilead","Drawing water at the well of Harod"],a:0,ref:"Judges 6:11",verse:"And there came an angel of the LORD, and sat under an oak which was in Ophrah, that pertained unto Joash the Abi-ezrite: and his son Gideon threshed wheat by the winepress, to hide it from the Midianites.",insight:"Gideon was hiding his wheat in a winepress, working in fear. God meets His servants in their hiding places, not only on mountaintops."},
  {q:"What did the angel call Gideon when he greeted him?",options:["Thou mighty man of valour","Thou fearful son of Joash","Thou keeper of the winepress","Thou least of Manasseh"],a:0,ref:"Judges 6:12",verse:"And the angel of the LORD appeared unto him, and said unto him, The LORD is with thee, thou mighty man of valour.",insight:"God called him a mighty man of valour while he was cowering at a winepress. God names you for what He will make you, not for what you feel like today."},
  {q:"What was Gideon's first reply to the angel's greeting?",options:["If the LORD be with us, why then is all this befallen us?","Blessed be the LORD who has sent thee","I am ready, send me now","Who art thou, my lord?"],a:0,ref:"Judges 6:13",verse:"And Gideon said unto him, Oh my Lord, if the LORD be with us, why then is all this befallen us? and where be all his miracles which our fathers told us of, saying, Did not the LORD bring us up from Egypt? but now the LORD hath forsaken us, and delivered us into the hands of the Midianites.",insight:"Gideon's honest question came from a hurting heart. God does not scold sincere questions; He answers them with a calling."},
  {q:"What did the LORD tell Gideon to do with the might he had?",options:["Go in this thy might, and thou shalt save Israel from the hand of the Midianites","Wait forty days and then gather an army","Flee to the mountains and hide","Offer a sacrifice and return home"],a:0,ref:"Judges 6:14",verse:"And the LORD looked upon him, and said, Go in this thy might, and thou shalt save Israel from the hand of the Midianites: have not I sent thee?",insight:"God sent Gideon in the strength he already had. You do not need to feel mighty first; obedience turns small might into deliverance."},
  {q:"Why did Gideon say he could not save Israel?",options:["His family was poor in Manasseh, and he was the least in his father's house","He was too old to fight","He had never held a sword","His tribe refused to follow him"],a:0,ref:"Judges 6:15",verse:"And he said unto him, Oh my Lord, wherewith shall I save Israel? behold, my family is poor in Manasseh, and I am the least in my father’s house.",insight:"Gideon listed his excuses: poor family, lowest rank. God specializes in calling the least, because then no one can steal His glory."},
  {q:"What promise did the LORD give Gideon for the battle?",options:["Surely I will be with thee, and thou shalt smite the Midianites as one man","I will send ten thousand angels before thee","The Midianites will flee before they see thee","Thou shalt not need to fight at all"],a:0,ref:"Judges 6:16",verse:"And the LORD said unto him, Surely I will be with thee, and thou shalt smite the Midianites as one man.",insight:"The whole promise was God's presence, not Gideon's power. 'I will be with thee' is still the greatest battle promise a believer can carry."},
  {q:"What sign did Gideon ask for before he would believe?",options:["Shew me a sign that thou talkest with me","Make the sun stand still","Let fire fall from heaven","Heal my withered hand"],a:0,ref:"Judges 6:17",verse:"And he said unto him, If now I have found grace in thy sight, then shew me a sign that thou talkest with me.",insight:"Gideon asked for a sign, and God patiently gave one. God is kind to doubters who want to be sure, as long as they are moving toward obedience."},
  {q:"What offering did Gideon prepare for the angel?",options:["A kid, and unleavened cakes of an ephah of flour","A bullock and two rams","A basket of firstfruits","A drink offering of wine"],a:0,ref:"Judges 6:19",verse:"And Gideon went in, and made ready a kid, and unleavened cakes of an ephah of flour: the flesh he put in a basket, and he put the broth in a pot, and brought it out unto him under the oak, and presented it.",insight:"Gideon brought a full meal, not a token gift. When we come before the Lord, wholehearted giving still speaks louder than leftovers."},
  {q:"How did the angel consume Gideon's offering?",options:["Fire rose up out of the rock and consumed the flesh and the cakes","Lightning fell from a clear sky","The angel breathed and it turned to ashes","The ground opened and swallowed it"],a:0,ref:"Judges 6:21",verse:"Then the angel of the LORD put forth the end of the staff that was in his hand, and touched the flesh and the unleavened cakes; and there rose up fire out of the rock, and consumed the flesh and the unleavened cakes. Then the angel of the LORD departed out of his sight.",insight:"Fire from the rock proved this was no ordinary visitor. God still answers honest seekers with unmistakable signs of His presence."},
  {q:"What did Gideon build after the angel departed, and what did he name it?",options:["An altar named Jehovahshalom, the LORD is peace","A tower named Mizpeh","A pillar named Ebenezer","A tent named Mahanaim"],a:0,ref:"Judges 6:24",verse:"Then Gideon built an altar there unto the LORD, and called it Jehovah-shalom: unto this day it is yet in Ophrah of the Abi-ezrites.",insight:"After trembling with fear, Gideon named the altar 'The LORD is Peace.' Meeting God turns our panic into peace; that is what His presence does."},
  {q:"What was Gideon commanded to do on the very night of his calling?",options:["Throw down the altar of Baal and cut down the grove by it","Leave Ophrah and never return","Number all the men of Manasseh","Build a wall around his father's house"],a:0,ref:"Judges 6:25",verse:"And it came to pass the same night, that the LORD said unto him, Take thy father’s young bullock, even the second bullock of seven years old, and throw down the altar of Baal that thy father hath, and cut down the grove that is by it:",insight:"Before Gideon could fight Midian, he had to cleanse his own father's house of idols. Victory abroad starts with repentance at home."},
  {q:"Why did Gideon tear down Baal's altar by night?",options:["Because he feared his father's household and the men of the city","Because the LORD commanded secrecy","Because the moon gave him light to work","Because the men of the city were asleep from wine"],a:0,ref:"Judges 6:27",verse:"Then Gideon took ten men of his servants, and did as the LORD had said unto him: and so it was, because he feared his father’s household, and the men of the city, that he could not do it by day, that he did it by night.",insight:"Gideon obeyed, but he obeyed by night because he was afraid. God accepts trembling obedience; courage often grows after we start."},
  {q:"What did Joash say when the men of the city demanded Gideon's death?",options:["If Baal be a god, let him plead for himself","My son has done no wrong","Take my life instead of his","Wait until the morning and judge him fairly"],a:0,ref:"Judges 6:31",verse:"And Joash said unto all that stood against him, Will ye plead for Baal? will ye save him? he that will plead for him, let him be put to death whilst it is yet morning: if he be a god, let him plead for himself, because one hath cast down his altar.",insight:"Joash turned the crowd's anger back on their idol: if Baal is real, let him defend himself. A god that needs your protection is no god at all."}
 ]},
{n:7, name:"Three Hundred Torches", seal:{ref:"Judges 7:20",verse:"And the three companies blew the trumpets, and brake the pitchers, and held the lamps in their left hands, and the trumpets in their right hands to blow withal: and they cried, The sword of the LORD, and of Gideon."},
 facts:["Did you know Gideon's army shrank from 32,000 to 300 men? God reduced it on purpose, so Israel could never boast that their own hand had saved them.","Did you know the Midianite soldier's dream was about a barley cake? Barley was the poor man's bread, a fitting picture of Gideon's small, unlikely army.","Did you know the 300 men carried trumpets, empty pitchers, and lamps? The pitchers hid the torches until the signal, when breaking them let the light blaze out at once."],
 questions:[
  {q:"Where did Gideon pitch his camp before the battle with Midian?",options:["Beside the well of Harod","In the valley of Jezreel","At the oak of Ophrah","Upon Mount Tabor"],a:0,ref:"Judges 7:1",verse:"Then Jerubbaal, who is Gideon, and all the people that were with him, rose up early, and pitched beside the well of Harod: so that the host of the Midianites were on the north side of them, by the hill of Moreh, in the valley.",insight:"The well of Harod means the well of trembling. God let Gideon camp at Trembling before He taught him to trust."},
  {q:"Why did the LORD say Gideon's army was too large?",options:["Lest Israel vaunt themselves against Him, saying, Mine own hand hath saved me","Because there was not enough food for them all","Because the Midianites would be afraid to fight","Because the trumpets would be too loud"],a:0,ref:"Judges 7:2",verse:"And the LORD said unto Gideon, The people that are with thee are too many for me to give the Midianites into their hands, lest Israel vaunt themselves against me, saying, Mine own hand hath saved me.",insight:"God shrinks our resources so we cannot steal His glory. When you win with too little, everyone knows who really won."},
  {q:"What test did Gideon first give to reduce the army?",options:["Whoever was fearful and afraid could return from Mount Gilead","Whoever had built a new house could go home","Whoever had planted a vineyard could depart","Whoever was the youngest son could leave"],a:0,ref:"Judges 7:3",verse:"Now therefore go to, proclaim in the ears of the people, saying, Whosoever is fearful and afraid, let him return and depart early from mount Gilead. And there returned of the people twenty and two thousand; and there remained ten thousand.",insight:"The fearful were sent home first. God would rather fight with a few brave hearts than a crowd of trembling ones."},
  {q:"How many men left after the first test?",options:["Twenty-two thousand returned, and ten thousand remained","Ten thousand returned, and twenty-two thousand remained","Five thousand returned, and twenty-seven thousand remained","Half of them left, half remained"],a:0,ref:"Judges 7:3",verse:"Now therefore go to, proclaim in the ears of the people, saying, Whosoever is fearful and afraid, let him return and depart early from mount Gilead. And there returned of the people twenty and two thousand; and there remained ten thousand.",insight:"Two thirds of the army walked away, and God called that progress. In God's arithmetic, subtraction can be the way to victory."},
  {q:"Where did the LORD tell Gideon to bring the remaining men for the second test?",options:["Down to the water","Up to the top of Mount Gilead","Into the valley of Jezreel","To the plain of the pillar"],a:0,ref:"Judges 7:4",verse:"And the LORD said unto Gideon, The people are yet too many; bring them down unto the water, and I will try them for thee there: and it shall be, that of whom I say unto thee, This shall go with thee, the same shall go with thee; and of whomsoever I say unto thee, This shall not go with thee, the same shall not go.",insight:"God tested them at the water, in an ordinary moment. Character shows most clearly in the small, everyday choices."},
  {q:"How did the chosen three hundred drink the water?",options:["They lapped it with their tongue, as a dog lappeth","They bowed down upon their knees to drink","They cupped it in their helmets","They lay flat and drank with reeds"],a:0,ref:"Judges 7:5",verse:"So he brought down the people unto the water: and the LORD said unto Gideon, Every one that lappeth of the water with his tongue, as a dog lappeth, him shalt thou set by himself; likewise every one that boweth down upon his knees to drink.",insight:"The three hundred stayed alert, lapping water with eyes up. God chose watchful men, ready for battle even while drinking."},
  {q:"How many men were chosen by the water test?",options:["Three hundred","Three thousand","Thirty thousand","Thirty"],a:0,ref:"Judges 7:6",verse:"And the number of them that lapped, putting their hand to their mouth, were three hundred men: but all the rest of the people bowed down upon their knees to drink water.",insight:"Three hundred out of thirty-two thousand. God loves impossible odds because they leave no doubt about who deserves the praise."},
  {q:"What did the LORD promise to do by the three hundred men?",options:["Save Israel and deliver the Midianites into Gideon's hand","Make them captains over thousands","Give them the land of the Midianites","Send them home in peace"],a:0,ref:"Judges 7:7",verse:"And the LORD said unto Gideon, By the three hundred men that lapped will I save you, and deliver the Midianites into thine hand: and let all the other people go every man unto his place.",insight:"God promised salvation through the few, not the many. His power is never measured by the size of your resources."},
  {q:"Whom did Gideon take with him when he went down to the Midianite camp by night?",options:["Phurah his servant","Joash his father","Jether his firstborn","Zebul the ruler"],a:0,ref:"Judges 7:10",verse:"But if thou fear to go down, go thou with Phurah thy servant down to the host:",insight:"Gideon took one trusted servant into the dark. Even the called of God need faithful company when fear presses in."},
  {q:"What did God tell Gideon he would hear in the enemy camp?",options:["Words that would strengthen his hands for the battle","The plans of the Midianite princes","The number of the enemy host","The complaints of the Amalekites"],a:0,ref:"Judges 7:11",verse:"And thou shalt hear what they say; and afterward shall thine hands be strengthened to go down unto the host. Then went he down with Phurah his servant unto the outside of the armed men that were in the host.",insight:"God sent Gideon to eavesdrop on purpose, to hear encouragement. God often places the exact word you need right where you are afraid to go."},
  {q:"How are the Midianites described as they lay in the valley?",options:["Like grasshoppers for multitude, with camels as the sand by the sea shore","Like lions crouching for the kill","Like a forest of spears","Like stars too many to count"],a:0,ref:"Judges 7:12",verse:"And the Midianites and the Amalekites and all the children of the east lay along in the valley like grasshoppers for multitude; and their camels were without number, as the sand by the sea side for multitude.",insight:"The enemy looked countless, like grasshoppers covering the valley. Sight says impossible; faith says the battle belongs to the Lord."},
  {q:"What did the Midianite soldier dream about in his dream?",options:["A cake of barley bread that tumbled into the host of Midian and smote a tent","A great sword falling from heaven","A flood sweeping away the camp","A lion devouring the captains"],a:0,ref:"Judges 7:13",verse:"And when Gideon was come, behold, there was a man that told a dream unto his fellow, and said, Behold, I dreamed a dream, and, lo, a cake of barley bread tumbled into the host of Midian, and came unto a tent, and smote it that it fell, and overturned it, that the tent lay along.",insight:"God put the dream in an enemy's sleep. He can speak through anyone, even those who oppose you, to confirm His promise."},
  {q:"How did the soldier's companion interpret the barley cake dream?",options:["This is nothing else save the sword of Gideon the son of Joash","This means famine will strike our camp","This means we should flee to the mountains","This means the barley harvest will fail"],a:0,ref:"Judges 7:14",verse:"And his fellow answered and said, This is nothing else save the sword of Gideon the son of Joash, a man of Israel: for into his hand hath God delivered Midian, and all the host.",insight:"The enemy already knew Gideon's name and feared him. Sometimes your adversaries believe God's promise about you before you do."},
  {q:"What did Gideon do when he heard the dream and its interpretation?",options:["He worshipped, and returned to his camp to rouse the army","He wept for joy and told no one","He sent messengers to make peace","He waited until morning to decide"],a:0,ref:"Judges 7:15",verse:"And it was so, when Gideon heard the telling of the dream, and the interpretation thereof, that he worshipped, and returned into the host of Israel, and said, Arise; for the LORD hath delivered into your hand the host of Midian.",insight:"Gideon's first response was worship, not war plans. Worship is the right answer to encouragement; it turns good news into courage."},
  {q:"Into how many companies did Gideon divide the three hundred men?",options:["Three companies","Ten companies","Twelve companies","Thirty companies"],a:0,ref:"Judges 7:16",verse:"And he divided the three hundred men into three companies, and he put a trumpet in every man’s hand, with empty pitchers, and lamps within the pitchers.",insight:"Three companies would surround the camp on every side. God's strategies are simple, but they always cover every angle."},
  {q:"What did every man carry in his hands for the night attack?",options:["A trumpet, an empty pitcher, and a lamp within the pitcher","A sword, a shield, and a spear","A bow, a quiver, and a torch","A sling, stones, and a staff"],a:0,ref:"Judges 7:16",verse:"And he divided the three hundred men into three companies, and he put a trumpet in every man’s hand, with empty pitchers, and lamps within the pitchers.",insight:"No swords, only trumpets and clay pitchers with hidden lamps. God armed them with noise and light, because the battle was His, not theirs."},
  {q:"What were the men commanded to cry when they broke the pitchers?",options:["The sword of the LORD, and of Gideon","The day of vengeance is come","For Israel and for freedom","Let God arise and scatter"],a:0,ref:"Judges 7:20",verse:"And the three companies blew the trumpets, and brake the pitchers, and held the lamps in their left hands, and the trumpets in their right hands to blow withal: and they cried, The sword of the LORD, and of Gideon.",insight:"Their battle cry named the LORD first and Gideon second. Put God's name before your own in every victory, and the victory stays His."},
  {q:"What happened in the Midianite camp when the trumpets blew and the pitchers broke?",options:["Every man stood in his place while the whole host ran, and cried, and fled","The Midianites surrendered at once","The Amalekites joined Gideon's side","The camp fell silent in amazement"],a:0,ref:"Judges 7:21",verse:"And they stood every man in his place round about the camp: and all the host ran, and cried, and fled.",insight:"The three hundred simply stood their ground while panic did the fighting. Sometimes obedience means standing still while God moves."},
  {q:"What did the LORD do to the Midianite army as the trumpets sounded?",options:["He set every man's sword against his fellow throughout the host","He sent hailstones upon them","He blinded their eyes","He stopped their hearts with fear"],a:0,ref:"Judges 7:22",verse:"And the three hundred blew the trumpets, and the LORD set every man’s sword against his fellow, even throughout all the host: and the host fled to Beth-shittah in Zererath, and to the border of Abel-meholah, unto Tabbath.",insight:"God turned their swords on each other. When God fights for you, your enemies' own weapons become their downfall."},
  {q:"Which two princes of Midian were captured, and where were they slain?",options:["Oreb upon the rock Oreb, and Zeeb at the winepress of Zeeb","Zebah at Karkor, and Zalmunna at Succoth","Oreb at Bethbarah, and Zeeb at the Jordan","Zebah upon Mount Tabor, and Zalmunna in the valley"],a:0,ref:"Judges 7:25",verse:"And they took two princes of the Midianites, Oreb and Zeeb; and they slew Oreb upon the rock Oreb, and Zeeb they slew at the winepress of Zeeb, and pursued Midian, and brought the heads of Oreb and Zeeb to Gideon on the other side Jordan.",insight:"The places of their death were named after them, a lasting memorial. God finishes what He starts; no enemy He has marked escapes His justice."}
 ]},
{n:8, name:"The Snare of the Ephod", seal:{ref:"Judges 8:23",verse:"And Gideon said unto them, I will not rule over you, neither shall my son rule over you: the LORD shall rule over you."},
 facts:["Did you know Gideon was offered the crown of Israel and refused it? He said, I will not rule over you, neither shall my son rule over you: the LORD shall rule over you.","Did you know the gold for Gideon's ephod weighed 1,700 shekels? That is roughly 43 pounds of gold gathered from the earrings of the fallen Midianites.","Did you know Gideon is also called Jerubbaal through most of this chapter? The name means let Baal plead, a reminder of the night he tore down Baal's altar."],
 questions:[
  {q:"Why did the men of Ephraim sharply chide Gideon after the victory?",options:["Because he had not called them to fight the Midianites at first","Because he had taken all the spoil for himself","Because he had made a league with the Amalekites","Because he had burned their city"],a:0,ref:"Judges 8:1",verse:"And the men of Ephraim said unto him, Why hast thou served us thus, that thou calledst us not, when thou wentest to fight with the Midianites? And they did chide with him sharply.",insight:"Ephraim complained about being left out of the glory. A soft answer turned their anger away; pride demands credit, but wisdom shares it."},
  {q:"How did Gideon calm the anger of the Ephraimites?",options:["He praised their capture of Oreb and Zeeb above his own victory","He offered them half of the spoil","He promised them the next battle","He rebuked them for their pride"],a:0,ref:"Judges 8:2",verse:"And he said unto them, What have I done now in comparison of you? Is not the gleaning of the grapes of Ephraim better than the vintage of Abi-ezer?",insight:"Gideon lifted up their part instead of defending his own. Honoring others' contributions defuses envy faster than any argument."},
  {q:"When Gideon came to the Jordan in pursuit, what was the state of his men?",options:["Faint, yet pursuing","Fresh and fully rested","Wounded and turning back","Hungry and ready to quit"],a:0,ref:"Judges 8:4",verse:"And Gideon came to Jordan, and passed over, he, and the three hundred men that were with him, faint, yet pursuing them.",insight:"Faint, yet pursuing: tired but still moving. God honors the weary who keep going more than the strong who never start."},
  {q:"What did Gideon ask of the men of Succoth?",options:["Loaves of bread for his weary men","Fresh horses for the pursuit","A guide through the wilderness","Water from their wells"],a:0,ref:"Judges 8:5",verse:"And he said unto the men of Succoth, Give, I pray you, loaves of bread unto the people that follow me; for they be faint, and I am pursuing after Zebah and Zalmunna, kings of Midian.",insight:"Gideon asked only for bread for his exhausted men. Leaders who ask for their people's needs, not their own comfort, lead well."},
  {q:"How did the princes of Succoth answer Gideon's request?",options:["Are the hands of Zebah and Zalmunna now in thine hand, that we should give bread?","We have no bread to spare in this famine","Go to Penuel, for they are richer than we","We will give bread when the battle is won"],a:0,ref:"Judges 8:6",verse:"And the princes of Succoth said, Are the hands of Zebah and Zalmunna now in thine hand, that we should give bread unto thine army?",insight:"Succoth refused to help until victory was certain. Refusing to stand with God's people in the hard part is a failure of its own."},
  {q:"What did Gideon threaten to do to the men of Succoth?",options:["Tear their flesh with the thorns of the wilderness and with briers","Burn their city with fire","Take their cattle as spoil","Drive them out of the land"],a:0,ref:"Judges 8:7",verse:"And Gideon said, Therefore when the LORD hath delivered Zebah and Zalmunna into mine hand, then I will tear your flesh with the thorns of the wilderness and with briers.",insight:"Gideon's threat was harsh, and he carried it out. Words spoken in anger become deeds we must answer for; guard your threats as well as your promises."},
  {q:"What did Gideon threaten against the tower of Penuel?",options:["When I come again in peace, I will break down this tower","I will set it on fire with the men inside","I will pull it down stone by stone tonight","I will give it to the Midianites"],a:0,ref:"Judges 8:9",verse:"And he spake also unto the men of Penuel, saying, When I come again in peace, I will break down this tower.",insight:"Penuel mocked him just as Succoth had. Gideon marked both places for judgment; those who despise God's servants will answer for it."},
  {q:"How many Midianites had already fallen before Gideon reached Karkor?",options:["A hundred and twenty thousand men that drew sword","Forty thousand horsemen","Ten thousand footmen","Three hundred chosen men"],a:0,ref:"Judges 8:10",verse:"Now Zebah and Zalmunna were in Karkor, and their hosts with them, about fifteen thousand men, all that were left of all the hosts of the children of the east: for there fell an hundred and twenty thousand men that drew sword.",insight:"One hundred and twenty thousand had fallen to three hundred men. The math of heaven never matches the math of earth."},
  {q:"Who were the two kings of Midian that Gideon pursued to Karkor?",options:["Zebah and Zalmunna","Oreb and Zeeb","Jether and Phurah","Gaal and Zebul"],a:0,ref:"Judges 8:12",verse:"And when Zebah and Zalmunna fled, he pursued after them, and took the two kings of Midian, Zebah and Zalmunna, and discomfited all the host.",insight:"The two kings fled while their army perished. Leaders who send others to die while they run will still face justice in the end."},
  {q:"How many elders and princes of Succoth did the young man describe to Gideon?",options:["Threescore and seventeen men","Seventy men","Fifty men","Thirty men"],a:0,ref:"Judges 8:14",verse:"And caught a young man of the men of Succoth, and enquired of him: and he described unto him the princes of Succoth, and the elders thereof, even threescore and seventeen men.",insight:"Seventy-seven names were written down, every one remembered. God keeps exact records; no act of scorn toward His people goes unnoticed."},
  {q:"What did Gideon do to the elders of Succoth when he returned?",options:["He taught them with the thorns of the wilderness and with briers","He forgave them freely","He made them carry his baggage","He banished them from the city"],a:0,ref:"Judges 8:16",verse:"And he took the elders of the city, and thorns of the wilderness and briers, and with them he taught the men of Succoth.",insight:"Gideon kept his harsh word against Succoth. Keeping your word matters, but this shows why we should be slow to speak threats in the first place."},
  {q:"What did Gideon do to the tower of Penuel and its men?",options:["He beat down the tower and slew the men of the city","He burned it with fire","He pulled it down and rebuilt it","He left it standing as a warning"],a:0,ref:"Judges 8:17",verse:"And he beat down the tower of Penuel, and slew the men of the city.",insight:"Penuel's tower fell as Gideon had sworn. Mocking God's deliverer proved fatal; contempt for God's work is never a safe game."},
  {q:"Who had slain Gideon's brothers at Tabor?",options:["Zebah and Zalmunna","Oreb and Zeeb","The men of Succoth","The Amalekites"],a:0,ref:"Judges 8:18",verse:"Then said he unto Zebah and Zalmunna, What manner of men were they whom ye slew at Tabor? And they answered, As thou art, so were they; each one resembled the children of a king.",insight:"The kings admitted killing Gideon's own brothers. This battle was personal as well as national; God weaves our private griefs into His public justice."},
  {q:"Why did Jether, Gideon's firstborn, not slay the captive kings?",options:["He feared, because he was yet a youth","His sword was broken","Gideon stopped him","The kings begged for mercy"],a:0,ref:"Judges 8:20",verse:"And he said unto Jether his firstborn, Up, and slay them. But the youth drew not his sword: for he feared, because he was yet a youth.",insight:"The youth feared to strike. Courage is not the absence of fear but action despite it; even Gideon's son had to learn what his father learned."},
  {q:"What did Gideon take from the slain kings' camels?",options:["The ornaments that were on their camels' necks","Their crowns of gold","Their swords of brass","Their signet rings"],a:0,ref:"Judges 8:21",verse:"Then Zebah and Zalmunna said, Rise thou, and fall upon us: for as the man is, so is his strength. And Gideon arose, and slew Zebah and Zalmunna, and took away the ornaments that were on their camels’ necks.",insight:"He stripped the royal ornaments from the camels. The symbols of Midian's pride became the spoil of God's victory."},
  {q:"What did the men of Israel ask Gideon to do after the victory?",options:["Rule over them, he and his son and his son's son","Build them a temple in Ophrah","Lead them back to Egypt","Divide the spoil equally"],a:0,ref:"Judges 8:22",verse:"Then the men of Israel said unto Gideon, Rule thou over us, both thou, and thy son, and thy son’s son also: for thou hast delivered us from the hand of Midian.",insight:"Victory made the people want a king. Success tempts us to crown the servant instead of the Master who sent him."},
  {q:"How did Gideon answer the offer of kingship?",options:["I will not rule over you, neither shall my son rule over you: the LORD shall rule over you","I will rule, but my son shall not","Let my son rule in my place","I will consider it after forty days"],a:0,ref:"Judges 8:23",verse:"And Gideon said unto them, I will not rule over you, neither shall my son rule over you: the LORD shall rule over you.",insight:"Gideon's greatest words: the LORD shall rule over you. He knew the throne was God's, and refusing it was his finest victory."},
  {q:"What one request did Gideon make of the people instead?",options:["That every man give him the earrings of his prey","That they build him a house in Ophrah","That they give him a tenth of the spoil","That they make his son a prince"],a:0,ref:"Judges 8:24",verse:"And Gideon said unto them, I would desire a request of you, that ye would give me every man the earrings of his prey. (For they had golden earrings, because they were Ishmaelites.)",insight:"One small request seemed harmless: just the earrings. Small compromises often open the door to great snares."},
  {q:"What did Gideon make from the gold, and what became of it?",options:["An ephod, which all Israel went a whoring after as a snare","A crown, which he wore in Ophrah","A trumpet, which was blown at feasts","An altar, which replaced Baal's"],a:0,ref:"Judges 8:27",verse:"And Gideon made an ephod thereof, and put it in his city, even in Ophrah: and all Israel went thither a whoring after it: which thing became a snare unto Gideon, and to his house.",insight:"The ephod became an idol and a snare to Gideon's own house. Good intentions can still build idols; even victories need watching."},
  {q:"How long did the country have quiet in the days of Gideon?",options:["Forty years","Twenty-three years","Eighteen years","Seventy years"],a:0,ref:"Judges 8:28",verse:"Thus was Midian subdued before the children of Israel, so that they lifted up their heads no more. And the country was in quietness forty years in the days of Gideon.",insight:"Forty years of peace followed Gideon's obedience. One person's faithfulness can shelter a whole generation in quiet."}
 ]},
{n:9, name:"The Bramble King", seal:{ref:"Judges 9:53",verse:"And a certain woman cast a piece of a millstone upon Abimelech’s head, and all to brake his skull."},
 facts:["Did you know Jotham's parable of the trees is one of the oldest fables in the Bible? The worthy trees, olive, fig, and vine, all refuse the crown, and only the worthless bramble accepts it.","Did you know Abimelech murdered all seventy of his brothers on a single stone? Only Jotham, the youngest, escaped by hiding.","Did you know Abimelech died begging his armourbearer to kill him? He could not bear that history would record a woman slew him."],
 questions:[
  {q:"Who was Abimelech's father?",options:["Jerubbaal, who is Gideon","Joash of the Abiezrites","Gaal the son of Ebed","Zebul the ruler of Shechem"],a:0,ref:"Judges 9:1",verse:"And Abimelech the son of Jerubbaal went to Shechem unto his mother’s brethren, and communed with them, and with all the family of the house of his mother’s father, saying,",insight:"Abimelech was Gideon's son by his concubine in Shechem. A great father's legacy can still produce a wicked son; each generation must choose."},
  {q:"To whom did Abimelech first pitch his claim to rule?",options:["His mother's brethren, the men of Shechem","The elders of Ophrah","The princes of Succoth","The men of Penuel"],a:0,ref:"Judges 9:1",verse:"And Abimelech the son of Jerubbaal went to Shechem unto his mother’s brethren, and communed with them, and with all the family of the house of his mother’s father, saying,",insight:"He started with his relatives, trading on family ties. Ambition often begins by using the people closest to us."},
  {q:"What choice did Abimelech offer the men of Shechem?",options:["That all threescore and ten sons of Jerubbaal reign over you, or that one reign over you","That he reign, or that Jotham reign","That Shechem be free, or that it serve Midian","That they pay tribute, or face war"],a:0,ref:"Judges 9:2",verse:"Speak, I pray you, in the ears of all the men of Shechem, Whether is better for you, either that all the sons of Jerubbaal, which are threescore and ten persons, reign over you, or that one reign over you? remember also that I am your bone and your flesh.",insight:"One ruler or seventy? He framed it as simplicity versus chaos. Beware of leaders who make cruelty sound like common sense."},
  {q:"How did Abimelech hire the vain and light persons who followed him?",options:["With threescore and ten pieces of silver from the house of Baalberith","With gold from the ephod","With promises of land in Gilead","With spoil taken from Thebez"],a:0,ref:"Judges 9:4",verse:"And they gave him threescore and ten pieces of silver out of the house of Baal-berith, wherewith Abimelech hired vain and light persons, which followed him.",insight:"Temple silver paid for murderers. Money taken from idolatry funded more evil; corrupt funds always find corrupt work."},
  {q:"Where did Abimelech slay his seventy brothers?",options:["Upon one stone in Ophrah","At the rock Oreb","In the tower of Shechem","By the well of Harod"],a:0,ref:"Judges 9:5",verse:"And he went unto his father’s house at Ophrah, and slew his brethren the sons of Jerubbaal, being threescore and ten persons, upon one stone: notwithstanding yet Jotham the youngest son of Jerubbaal was left; for he hid himself.",insight:"Seventy brothers on one stone: ambition's first altar is always family. Power gained by murder is never truly power."},
  {q:"Which brother escaped Abimelech's slaughter?",options:["Jotham, the youngest son of Jerubbaal","Abimelech's armourbearer","Joash, his grandfather","Phurah, the servant"],a:0,ref:"Judges 9:5",verse:"And he went unto his father’s house at Ophrah, and slew his brethren the sons of Jerubbaal, being threescore and ten persons, upon one stone: notwithstanding yet Jotham the youngest son of Jerubbaal was left; for he hid himself.",insight:"Jotham hid himself and lived to speak the truth. God preserves witnesses even in the darkest massacres."},
  {q:"Where did the men of Shechem make Abimelech king?",options:["By the plain of the pillar that was in Shechem","On the top of Mount Gerizim","In the house of Baalberith","At the gate of Arumah"],a:0,ref:"Judges 9:6",verse:"And all the men of Shechem gathered together, and all the house of Millo, and went, and made Abimelech king, by the plain of the pillar that was in Shechem.",insight:"They crowned a murderer by a sacred pillar. A ceremony cannot make a crime legitimate, no matter how official it looks."},
  {q:"From where did Jotham speak his parable to the men of Shechem?",options:["The top of Mount Gerizim","The tower of Shechem","The plain of the pillar","The gate of the city"],a:0,ref:"Judges 9:7",verse:"And when they told it to Jotham, he went and stood in the top of mount Gerizim, and lifted up his voice, and cried, and said unto them, Hearken unto me, ye men of Shechem, that God may hearken unto you.",insight:"Jotham spoke from a mountaintop, out of arrow range. Truth sometimes must be spoken from a safe distance, but it still must be spoken."},
  {q:"In Jotham's parable, which tree was first asked to reign over the trees?",options:["The olive tree","The fig tree","The vine","The cedar"],a:0,ref:"Judges 9:8",verse:"The trees went forth on a time to anoint a king over them; and they said unto the olive tree, Reign thou over us.",insight:"The olive was asked first, the most honored tree. True worth is often too busy bearing fruit to chase a crown."},
  {q:"Why did the olive tree refuse to be king?",options:["Should I leave my fatness, wherewith by me they honour God and man?","Because the fig tree was more beautiful","Because it feared the bramble","Because its roots were too deep"],a:0,ref:"Judges 9:9",verse:"But the olive tree said unto them, Should I leave my fatness, wherewith by me they honour God and man, and go to be promoted over the trees?",insight:"The olive would not trade its fruitful calling for a title. Never abandon the work God gave you just to be promoted."},
  {q:"Why did the fig tree refuse the crown?",options:["Should I forsake my sweetness and my good fruit?","Because the olive had refused first","Because the trees would not obey it","Because its branches were too weak"],a:0,ref:"Judges 9:11",verse:"But the fig tree said unto them, Should I forsake my sweetness, and my good fruit, and go to be promoted over the trees?",insight:"The fig tree knew its sweetness mattered more than a throne. Usefulness to God and man outweighs any position."},
  {q:"Why did the vine refuse to reign?",options:["Should I leave my wine, which cheereth God and man?","Because the fig tree mocked it","Because it had no thorns to defend itself","Because the harvest was not yet come"],a:0,ref:"Judges 9:13",verse:"And the vine said unto them, Should I leave my wine, which cheereth God and man, and go to be promoted over the trees?",insight:"The vine cheered God and man with its fruit. Joy-giving service is a higher calling than ruling over others."},
  {q:"Which worthless plant finally accepted the crown in the parable?",options:["The bramble","The thistle","The nettle","The thorn bush of the field"],a:0,ref:"Judges 9:14",verse:"Then said all the trees unto the bramble, Come thou, and reign over us.",insight:"Only the bramble, good for nothing but scratching, wanted the throne. Empty people chase titles; fruitful people chase their calling."},
  {q:"What threat did the bramble make in Jotham's parable?",options:["Let fire come out of the bramble and devour the cedars of Lebanon","Let the trees be cut down for timber","Let the olive be uprooted","Let the vine be trodden under foot"],a:0,ref:"Judges 9:15",verse:"And the bramble said unto the trees, If in truth ye anoint me king over you, then come and put your trust in my shadow: and if not, let fire come out of the bramble, and devour the cedars of Lebanon.",insight:"The bramble's first act as king was threatening fire. Worthless rulers govern by fear, because they have nothing else to offer."},
  {q:"Where did Jotham flee after delivering his parable?",options:["To Beer, where he dwelt for fear of Abimelech","To Ophrah, to his father's house","To the wilderness of Judah","Across the Jordan to Gilead"],a:0,ref:"Judges 9:21",verse:"And Jotham ran away, and fled, and went to Beer, and dwelt there, for fear of Abimelech his brother.",insight:"Jotham ran to Beer and stayed hidden. Speaking truth to tyrants is brave; surviving to tell it again is wisdom."},
  {q:"How long did Abimelech reign over Israel?",options:["Three years","Forty years","Twenty-two years","Seven years"],a:0,ref:"Judges 9:22",verse:"When Abimelech had reigned three years over Israel,",insight:"Three short years: that was all his blood-bought kingdom lasted. Evil reigns are always shorter than they promise."},
  {q:"What did God send between Abimelech and the men of Shechem?",options:["An evil spirit, so that they dealt treacherously with him","A plague upon their cattle","A famine in their fields","A prophet to rebuke them"],a:0,ref:"Judges 9:23",verse:"Then God sent an evil spirit between Abimelech and the men of Shechem; and the men of Shechem dealt treacherously with Abimelech:",insight:"God set the partners in crime against each other. Wicked alliances always rot from within; sin betrays its own friends."},
  {q:"Who became the rival leader that the men of Shechem trusted against Abimelech?",options:["Gaal the son of Ebed","Zebul the ruler of the city","Jotham the son of Jerubbaal","Phurah the servant"],a:0,ref:"Judges 9:26",verse:"And Gaal the son of Ebed came with his brethren, and went over to Shechem: and the men of Shechem put their confidence in him.",insight:"Shechem traded one troublemaker for another in Gaal. Replacing a bad leader with a worse one is no deliverance at all."},
  {q:"What did Abimelech do to the city of Shechem after he captured it?",options:["He slew the people, beat down the city, and sowed it with salt","He burned it and built a tower","He gave it to Zebul to rule","He made it tributary to Ophrah"],a:0,ref:"Judges 9:45",verse:"And Abimelech fought against the city all that day; and he took the city, and slew the people that was therein, and beat down the city, and sowed it with salt.",insight:"He sowed the ruins with salt, a curse of barrenness. Cruelty that destroys a whole city will itself be destroyed."},
  {q:"How did the thousand people in the tower of Shechem die?",options:["Abimelech set the hold on fire over them","They were slain with the sword","They starved during the siege","The tower collapsed upon them"],a:0,ref:"Judges 9:49",verse:"And all the people likewise cut down every man his bough, and followed Abimelech, and put them to the hold, and set the hold on fire upon them; so that all the men of the tower of Shechem died also, about a thousand men and women.",insight:"A thousand burned in the tower, the very curse Jotham had spoken. God's warnings are not empty words; they come to pass."}
 ]},
{n:10, name:"Tola, Jair, and the Cry", seal:{ref:"Judges 10:16",verse:"And they put away the strange gods from among them, and served the LORD: and his soul was grieved for the misery of Israel."},
 facts:["Did you know Jair had thirty sons who rode on thirty donkeys and owned thirty cities? In those days, riding a donkey colt was a sign of honor, like a royal motorcade.","Did you know Israel served seven different groups of gods at once in Judges 10? Baalim, Ashtaroth, and the gods of Syria, Zidon, Moab, Ammon, and the Philistines.","Did you know God once told Israel, I will deliver you no more? It was the strongest warning He ever gave them, yet their true repentance still moved His heart."],
 questions:[
  {q:"Who was the first judge to arise after Abimelech?",options:["Tola the son of Puah, the son of Dodo","Jair the Gileadite","Jephthah the Gileadite","Ibzan of Bethlehem"],a:0,ref:"Judges 10:1",verse:"And after Abimelech there arose to defend Israel Tola the son of Puah, the son of Dodo, a man of Issachar; and he dwelt in Shamir in mount Ephraim.",insight:"Tola rose quietly to defend Israel, with no drama recorded. Faithful service does not need a spotlight to matter to God."},
  {q:"Of which tribe was Tola?",options:["Issachar","Manasseh","Gad","Ephraim"],a:0,ref:"Judges 10:1",verse:"And after Abimelech there arose to defend Israel Tola the son of Puah, the son of Dodo, a man of Issachar; and he dwelt in Shamir in mount Ephraim.",insight:"Tola came from Issachar, a tribe known for understanding the times. God raises deliverers from every tribe, not just the famous ones."},
  {q:"How long did Tola judge Israel?",options:["Twenty-three years","Twenty-two years","Forty years","Eighteen years"],a:0,ref:"Judges 10:2",verse:"And he judged Israel twenty and three years, and died, and was buried in Shamir.",insight:"Twenty-three years of steady, unrecorded faithfulness. Most of God's best work in a life never makes the headlines."},
  {q:"Who judged Israel after Tola?",options:["Jair, a Gileadite","Jephthah, his kinsman","Elon, a Zebulonite","Abdon, the son of Hillel"],a:0,ref:"Judges 10:3",verse:"And after him arose Jair, a Gileadite, and judged Israel twenty and two years.",insight:"Jair followed Tola in the quiet succession of judges. God's work continues through ordinary handoffs, not only through heroes."},
  {q:"How many sons did Jair have, and what did they ride?",options:["Thirty sons that rode on thirty ass colts","Twenty sons that rode on horses","Seventy sons that rode on mules","Twelve sons that rode on camels"],a:0,ref:"Judges 10:4",verse:"And he had thirty sons that rode on thirty ass colts, and they had thirty cities, which are called Havoth-jair unto this day, which are in the land of Gilead.",insight:"Thirty sons on thirty donkey colts showed Jair's honor and prosperity. Blessings are meant to be shared with the next generation."},
  {q:"What were Jair's thirty cities called?",options:["Havothjair","The towns of Jair","Bashanhavoth","The villages of Gilead"],a:0,ref:"Judges 10:4",verse:"And he had thirty sons that rode on thirty ass colts, and they had thirty cities, which are called Havoth-jair unto this day, which are in the land of Gilead.",insight:"His cities carried his name, Havothjair, the towns of Jair. A godly legacy outlives the man who built it."},
  {q:"How long did Jair judge Israel?",options:["Twenty-two years","Twenty-three years","Forty years","Eight years"],a:0,ref:"Judges 10:3",verse:"And after him arose Jair, a Gileadite, and judged Israel twenty and two years.",insight:"Twenty-two years of judging, then burial in Camon. Faithful finishers matter as much as famous starters."},
  {q:"What evil did Israel do again after Jair?",options:["They served Baalim and Ashtaroth and the gods of the nations around them","They made a molten calf in Bethel","They refused to keep the Sabbath","They built high places to the sun"],a:0,ref:"Judges 10:6",verse:"And the children of Israel did evil again in the sight of the LORD, and served Baalim, and Ashtaroth, and the gods of Syria, and the gods of Zidon, and the gods of Moab, and the gods of the children of Ammon, and the gods of the Philistines, and forsook the LORD, and served not him.",insight:"They collected gods like souvenirs, seven kinds at once. Divided worship always leads to divided hearts."},
  {q:"Into whose hands did the LORD sell Israel this time?",options:["The Philistines and the children of Ammon","The Midianites and the Amalekites","The Moabites and the Edomites","The Syrians and the Zidonians"],a:0,ref:"Judges 10:7",verse:"And the anger of the LORD was hot against Israel, and he sold them into the hands of the Philistines, and into the hands of the children of Ammon.",insight:"Two enemies at once, east and west. When we forsake God, troubles rarely come one at a time."},
  {q:"How long did the oppression of that year last?",options:["Eighteen years","Twenty years","Seven years","Forty years"],a:0,ref:"Judges 10:8",verse:"And that year they vexed and oppressed the children of Israel: eighteen years, all the children of Israel that were on the other side Jordan in the land of the Amorites, which is in Gilead.",insight:"Eighteen years of vexation wore them down. God sometimes lets the pressure build until our idols finally break."},
  {q:"Which tribes did the Ammonites cross the Jordan to fight?",options:["Judah, Benjamin, and the house of Ephraim","Issachar, Zebulun, and Naphtali","Gad, Reuben, and half Manasseh","Dan, Asher, and Simeon"],a:0,ref:"Judges 10:9",verse:"Moreover the children of Ammon passed over Jordan to fight also against Judah, and against Benjamin, and against the house of Ephraim; so that Israel was sore distressed.",insight:"Ammon struck at the heartland tribes, not just the border. Sin's consequences eventually reach the center of our lives, not only the edges."},
  {q:"What did Israel confess when they cried unto the LORD?",options:["We have sinned, for we have forsaken our God and served Baalim","We have broken the covenant of our fathers","We have not obeyed the voice of the prophets","We have trusted in our own strength"],a:0,ref:"Judges 10:10",verse:"And the children of Israel cried unto the LORD, saying, We have sinned against thee, both because we have forsaken our God, and also served Baalim.",insight:"Their confession named the exact sin: forsaking God for Baalim. True repentance names the idol; vague sorrow changes nothing."},
  {q:"Which past deliverances did the LORD remind them of?",options:["From the Egyptians, Amorites, Ammonites, Philistines, Zidonians, Amalekites, and Maonites","From Pharaoh and Sihon only","From Midian and the Midianite kings","From the Canaanites at Jericho"],a:0,ref:"Judges 10:11",verse:"And the LORD said unto the children of Israel, Did not I deliver you from the Egyptians, and from the Amorites, from the children of Ammon, and from the Philistines?",insight:"God listed rescue after rescue they had forgotten. Remembering His past mercies is the cure for present despair."},
  {q:"What startling answer did the LORD give to their cry?",options:["Yet ye have forsaken me: wherefore I will deliver you no more","I have heard your cry and will send a judge","Return to me and I will return to you","Your sins are forgiven, go in peace"],a:0,ref:"Judges 10:13",verse:"Yet ye have forsaken me, and served other gods: wherefore I will deliver you no more.",insight:"'I will deliver you no more' is the sternest sentence in Judges. God let them feel the full weight of their choice before showing mercy."},
  {q:"Where did the LORD tell them to go for help instead?",options:["Go and cry unto the gods which ye have chosen; let them deliver you","Go to the Philistines for protection","Go back to Egypt for horses","Go to the high places and offer"],a:0,ref:"Judges 10:14",verse:"Go and cry unto the gods which ye have chosen; let them deliver you in the time of your tribulation.",insight:"God told them to ask their idols for help. Idols always fail in the day of trouble; that failure is meant to bring us home."},
  {q:"How did Israel answer the LORD's refusal?",options:["We have sinned: do unto us whatsoever seemeth good unto thee; deliver us only","We will serve the LORD from this day forever","We will destroy all our idols this very night","We will offer a thousand burnt offerings"],a:0,ref:"Judges 10:15",verse:"And the children of Israel said unto the LORD, We have sinned: do thou unto us whatsoever seemeth good unto thee; deliver us only, we pray thee, this day.",insight:"They stopped bargaining and surrendered: do whatever seems good to Thee. Surrender, not negotiation, is the doorway back to God."},
  {q:"What did Israel do to prove their repentance was real?",options:["They put away the strange gods from among them and served the LORD","They built a new altar at Mizpeh","They fasted for seven days","They sent gifts to the priests"],a:0,ref:"Judges 10:16",verse:"And they put away the strange gods from among them, and served the LORD: and his soul was grieved for the misery of Israel.",insight:"They put away the idols, not just the guilt. Repentance that keeps the idol is only regret wearing a disguise."},
  {q:"How did the LORD respond when He saw their misery?",options:["His soul was grieved for the misery of Israel","He turned His face away from them","He sent a prophet to warn them again","He waited another year"],a:0,ref:"Judges 10:16",verse:"And they put away the strange gods from among them, and served the LORD: and his soul was grieved for the misery of Israel.",insight:"His soul was grieved for their misery. God's heart bends toward the truly repentant; His anger never outlasts our return."},
  {q:"Where did the children of Ammon gather, and where did Israel assemble?",options:["Ammon encamped in Gilead, and Israel assembled at Mizpeh","Ammon encamped at Bethbarah, and Israel at the Jordan","Ammon encamped in Moab, and Israel at Gilgal","Ammon encamped at Rabbah, and Israel at Shechem"],a:0,ref:"Judges 10:17",verse:"Then the children of Ammon were gathered together, and encamped in Gilead. And the children of Israel assembled themselves together, and encamped in Mizpeh.",insight:"Both armies gathered, and the stage was set for Jephthah. When God is ready to act, He lines up every piece in its place."},
  {q:"What offer did the princes of Gilead make to whoever would lead the fight?",options:["He shall be head over all the inhabitants of Gilead","He shall have half the spoil","He shall marry the high priest's daughter","He shall rule for forty years"],a:0,ref:"Judges 10:18",verse:"And the people and princes of Gilead said one to another, What man is he that will begin to fight against the children of Ammon? he shall be head over all the inhabitants of Gilead.",insight:"They offered leadership to whoever would fight. God often promotes the willing; availability opens doors that ability alone cannot."}
 ]}
]);
